+# Summary: in-repo parser replaces `inilite`
+
+## What changed
+
+- `confkit/parse.py`: `inilite` is gone. `loads()` and `load()` are now a full parser for `docs/FORMAT.md`: lines and line endings, comments, names, sections, dotted keys, quoted strings with escapes, multi-line values, typed scalars, lists, `@include` with cycle detection, and the duplicate/conflict rules. Errors are `ParseError` with `message`, `lineno` and `source`.
+- `tests/test_format.py` (new): 49 tests for the rules the existing tests do not cover. Existing tests are unchanged.
+- No other files changed. `vendor/` was not opened, imported or copied from. No dependencies were added.
+
+## What I verified
+
+- `python3 -m unittest`: 62 tests run, all pass (13 original, 49 new). Before the change, the suite errored at import.
+- `python3 -m unittest` also covers `python -m confkit PATH --get server.port` (exit 0, prints `9000`), a missing key (exit 2, `no such key: ...`), and a duplicate key (exit 1, `...:2: duplicate key: a`), all through subprocess.
+- Spot checks run by hand: `x = a;b` gives `"a;b"`; `[a]#c` is an invalid section header; `k = "x"#c` is unexpected text after string; `h =` followed by a comment line and `'v'  # c` gives `"v"`.
+- `git status`: only `confkit/parse.py` is modified and `tests/test_format.py` is added, plus this file.
+
+## Not verified, or uncertain
+
+- **Interpretations where the spec was silent or ambiguous:**
+  - `@include"x"` with no space before the quote is accepted.
+  - Comment detection in plain (unquoted) values is not quote-aware, so `a = say "x # y"` gives `say "x`.
+  - Inside a list, `#` or `;` starts a comment only after whitespace. `[1,#c` makes `#c` an item token.
+  - A backslash at the end of a quoted line gives `unterminated string`, not `invalid escape`.
+  - A quoted value followed by continuation lines gives `unexpected text after string`.
+  - `[1 2]` is one token `"1 2"`, not an error. Only quoted or nested items need a comma between them.
+  - Error messages add `: detail` after the required prefix, e.g. `duplicate key: a.b`.
+- **Not covered by the spec:** a root file with invalid UTF-8 in `load()` raises `UnicodeDecodeError`, not `ParseError`. An included file with invalid UTF-8 gives `cannot read include`.
+- **No reference comparison:** nothing was checked against the original `inilite` behavior or any other parser. I did not fuzz the parser. The tests cover the spec as I read it, so a misreading would pass them.
+- **Manual CLI run:** I did not run `python -m confkit` by hand on a file. A scratch file under `/tmp` was blocked by the workspace guard. The subprocess tests exercise the same paths.
